From ada0579dc7712643a6f0a8a1a68c0457f536257f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 15:36:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(deepseek-v4.1): pick the smallest prepared capacity for
 GEMV projections

The unquantized V4.1 projections (the MoE router gate, the indexer head
weights and key projection, and the compressor projection) prepare one BF16
GEMV plan per row capacity: the CUDA-graph capture sizes and the batch
token limit. They looked up the plan for the exact row count and otherwise
fell back to the largest capacity. Each capacity selects its own backend:
the 4096-row router gate plan is the TMA prefill kernel, the 20-row plan a
SIMT GEMV, and they round differently. An eager batch between capture
sizes, such as a 19-token prefill alone, therefore ran the prefill kernel,
while the same rows padded into a captured batch ran the SIMT plan, and the
router logits, routing weights and routed output of those rows depended on
what else was scheduled.

Take the smallest prepared capacity at or above the row count instead, as
the block-FP8 linears already do. A plain loop rather than bisect keeps the
projection traceable with torch.compile(fullgraph=True). Batches above the
largest capture size still run the largest plan.
